>
> You mean why you shouldn't run
>
> Correct ~ newvariable+Syntax+Animacy+(1|Part.name) +(1|Item)?
>
> That's because your new variable creates all unique combinations of Syntax
> and Animacy (4). The resulting three degrees of freedom capture the same
> information as Animacy + Syntax + Animacy:Syntax.
>
> As it stands it looks like another way of rehashing the original final
>> model with all interactions defined and called.
>>
>> If I try this solution without the main effects it works fine and all
>> possible pairwise interactions are shown, which is very useful but I'm not
>> entirely sure is foolproof.
>>
> It's a full battery post-hoc test ;). you might want to adjust your alpha
> criterion in order to avoid anti-conservativity.  I haven't been using the
> multcomp package, so I don't know whether it already does that. I assume
> though that it does, as this would strike me as the main contributions such
> a package makes.
>
>
>> Another issue I have is with the test type fed to mcp. Can I use Turkey
>> to evaluate log odds coefficients?
>>
> Tukey in this context seems to mean only that you make all (6) pairwise
> comparisons. But the question is how the test is conducted. If it is
> conducted under the assumption of homogeneity of variance, this assumption
> is likely violated. It seems p11 in the multcomp vignette describes
> alternative ways to estimate the variance-covariance matrix that avoid this
> the homogeneity assumption.
>
> Florian
